    "result_text": "Full suite green: **64 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI redesigned the owner identity key and duplicate detection as specified.\n\n**New identity key** (`OwnerIdentityKey.of`): now the lower-case SHA-256 hex digest (64 chars) over `normalizedTelephone + '|' + lowerEmail + '|' + soundex(lastName)`, replacing the old plaintext `telephone|email|householdId`. Added a `Soundex` utility (standard American Soundex, with the H/W-transparent and vowel-reset rules) to encode the last name.\n\n**Duplicate detection (409)** (`CheckOwnerIdentityUnique`): now rejects on the single identity-key match only. I removed the separate household-duplicate block (the `householdId`-based ground and the `sharesHousehold`/`hasDistinguishingEmail` logic). It still skips soft-deleted owners, and the email-domain blocklist still runs first upstream (`RejectDisposableEmailDomain`).\n\n**Soft match** (`AssignPossibleDuplicate`): now triggers when the `identityKey` differs but `soundex(lastName)` and postcode both match (previously keyed on lastName + postcode + differing telephone). The lowest-id match wins; declared household members (`sharesHousehold`) and soft-deleted owners are still excluded.\n\nBecause the telephone is now inside the key, two owners with the same last name and postcode but different telephones get different keys \u2014 so they're created as a soft match rather than rejected with 409, exactly as the spec (and `Cp10Tests`) requires.\n\n`householdId` (via `AssignHousehold`) is retained since it still drives household-size counts and membership rules \u2014 it's just no longer part of duplicate detection. I also updated the stale javadocs/YAML comments to match the new behaviour.",
